  text="ESA's Trace Gas Orbiter (TGO) has found what ESA's blog calls \"signs of young volcanism on present-day Mars\". Its Atmospheric Chemistry Suite (ACS) instrument keeps detecting hydrogen chloride (HCl), a gas that comes out of volcanic vents on Earth, at about two parts per billion. The study traces it to the northern flanks of Alba Mons, a huge, low volcano, where TGO's CaSSIS camera saw fresh cracks and white spots that may be deposits around vents (an area about 20 by 20 km near 64.6°N, 274.4°E, imaged 3 February 2023). The gas shows up mostly in early northern summer; the team thinks ice and frozen ground hold it in during winter. The finding suggests magma may still be warm under Mars, but it is not proof of an eruption: no lava flow or eruption has ever been seen on Mars. Mège says hot-water systems underground could exist but would not have to host life. TGO's project scientist Colin Wilson told NASASpaceFlight the findings \"may prove to be TGO's greatest success yet\".",
