 dict(when="2026-11-04 01:42", who="Rocket Lab on X (@RocketLab), the company that built both ESCAPADE spacecraft; Rocket Lab is also the company protesting NASA's Mars relay award at GAO", kind="interested source (the spacecraft's builder, promoting its own work)", where="x.com (posts at 04:45 Israel time on 3 November and 01:20 to 01:42 Israel time on 4 November)",
  text="Rocket Lab built ESCAPADE's two orbiters, \"Blue\" and \"Gold\", for NASA and UC Berkeley. On 3 November it wrote that the trans-Mars injection (the engine burn that sends a craft from Earth toward Mars) is coming, with Blue's burn on Saturday 7 November and Gold's on Monday 9 November, the same dates NASA gave on 2 November. In a series of posts overnight it said the two near-identical spacecraft use its \"Explorer\" design, a platform built for large changes in speed on trips between planets, and weigh 535 kg each when fueled and 209 kg each dry. It also said they went \"from contract to complete in 3.5 years\", were shipped in August 2024 and launched in November 2025, and that the trip to Mars orbit \"takes the next big leap in three days\".",
  evidence="The dates match NASA's ESCAPADE blog (2 Nov). The 535 kg and 209 kg masses and the 13 November 2025 launch match NASA's ESCAPADE mission page. The August 2024 shipping date and the \"3.5 years\" figure are Rocket Lab's own [unconfirmed]; Rocket Lab's mission page says it was picked to build the spacecraft in November 2020.", url="https://x.com/RocketLab/status/2117448175271501907", link_text="open Rocket Lab's post, 3 Nov", other="", links=[L("Rocket Lab on X: mass, 4 Nov","https://x.com/RocketLab/status/2117759817955832247"),L("Rocket Lab on X: \"3.5 years\", 4 Nov","https://x.com/RocketLab/status/2117764388077903978"),L("Rocket Lab ESCAPADE mission page","https://rocketlabcorp.com/missions/escapade/"),L("NASA ESCAPADE mission page","https://science.nasa.gov/mission/escapade/")]),
 dict(when="2026-10-19", who="ESA, \"A Mars-velous decade: Celebrating ten years of TGO\" (ESA operations news)", kind="official (ESA)", where="esa.int (dated 19 October; first seen in our 4 November check, in ESA's operations news feed, which earlier checks did not read)",
  text="ESA marked ten years since its ExoMars Trace Gas Orbiter (TGO) reached Mars on 19 October 2016 at 15:24 UTC. The same day its small Schiaparelli lander was lost because a software problem shut its descent engines off too early; ESA says the lessons were applied to the Rosalind Franklin rover. From about 400 km up, TGO studies trace gases (gases present in tiny amounts). It has not detected methane, despite earlier reports of methane on Mars by other missions; it made the first detection of hydrogen chloride in Mars's air; it has measured water loss; its camera has mapped more than 10% of the surface in four colors, partly to study landing sites for Rosalind Franklin; and it has monitored radiation and mapped ice under the ground. It is also a relay for messages between Earth and the surface. Flight software sent up in 2024 limits use of its gyroscopes (the sensors that track which way it points) to save them for Rosalind Franklin's arrival. TGO can now store data from the surface during solar conjunction (when the Sun sits between Earth and Mars and blocks contact) and send it later. Its surface radio is now also used with Mars Express to study the atmosphere. The story gives no end date for the mission.",
  evidence="ESA's own story, quoting Silvia Sangiorgi (head of ESA's Mars mission operations unit), Colin Wilson (TGO project scientist), Peter Schmitz (spacecraft operations manager) and Jorge Vago (ExoMars project scientist). The 19 October 2016 arrival matches ESA's TGO facts-and-figures page of 15 October.", url="https://www.esa.int/Enabling_Support/Operations/A_Mars-velous_decade_Celebrating_ten_years_of_TGO", link_text="open the ESA story", other="", links=[L("ESA: TGO facts and figures","https://www.esa.int/ESA_Multimedia/Images/2026/10/Trace_Gas_Orbiter_Facts_and_figures")]),
