 dict(when="2026-11-02 21:11", who="NASA, ESCAPADE mission blog, \"NASA's Twin ESCAPADE Spacecraft to Slingshot Past Earth on Way to Mars\", by Vanessa Thomas (NASA Goddard)", kind="official (NASA)", where="science.nasa.gov (posted 2:11 PM U.S. Eastern time on 2 November; first seen in our 3 November check)",
  text="NASA now gives the dates and times itself. Each of the two ESCAPADE orbiters will fly close past Earth and fire its engine at the closest point, a \"powered flyby\" (using Earth's gravity and an engine burn together, also called an Oberth maneuver), which NASA says no Mars-bound spacecraft has done successfully before. The first orbiter's burn begins at 4:51 AM U.S. Eastern time on Saturday 7 November (11:51 AM Israel time, our own conversion); the second at 5:51 AM Eastern on Monday 9 November (12:51 PM Israel time). Each \"trans-Mars injection burn\" (the push that sends a craft from Earth toward Mars) lasts about 9 minutes and raises its speed from about 6.3 to about 6.7 miles per second (about 10.1 to 10.8 km per second, our own arithmetic). At closest approach the orbiters will be 600 to 1,100 miles (about 970 to 1,770 km) above the ground. After a 10-month cruise they reach Mars on 7 and 9 September 2027. NASA adds that during the year-long loop near Earth the pair flew through a distant part of Earth's magnetotail (the part of Earth's magnetic field stretched out behind it by the solar wind) that no spacecraft had sampled directly, with results to come. Skywatchers with telescopes may see them as faint moving dots: from the western U.S. in the hours before each burn, and from southern Australia during the burns.",
  evidence="NASA's own mission blog; it matches the 7 and 9 November dates Eos reported on 27 October, so those dates are no longer marked unconfirmed. Eos said the burn would last 9.5 minutes; NASA says about 9 minutes. Berkeley's mission-design page still says only \"November 2026\".", url="https://science.nasa.gov/blogs/escapade/2026/11/02/nasas-twin-escapade-spacecraft-to-slingshot-past-earth-on-way-to-mars/", link_text="open NASA's ESCAPADE blog, 2 Nov", other="", links=[L("Eos, 27 Oct","https://eos.org/articles/twin-spacecraft-ready-to-head-for-a-martian-escapade"),L("Berkeley ESCAPADE mission design","https://escapade.ssl.berkeley.edu/mission-design/")]),
