  text="Nothing new today on these, so only their checked dates change: NASA news releases (newest Mars item still the 29 October meteorite release; still no NASA story on the CHAPEA exit) and JPL news (read through JPL's news feed; newest 29 October), the Perseverance team blog (newest still \"Tinga's Tale\", 26 October), NASA's Perseverance raw-image feed (newest still sol 2024, 30 October) and traverse file (still ends at sol 2022, so the distance is unchanged), the Curiosity team blog (newest still sols 5043-5049, 28 October; no new drill hole reported), NASA's Mars Future Plan page (last updated 23 June; no low-cost Mars mission solicitation seen [unconfirmed]), NASA's and Berkeley's ESCAPADE pages (still \"fall of 2026\" and \"November 2026\" for the Mars departure burn, so the 7 and 9 November dates still rest on Eos alone [unconfirmed]), ESA's science and exploration news (no Mars item since 20 October), JAXA's English releases (newest still 21 October) and the MMX blog (newest still 30 October), CNES on X (one post on 1 November congratulating planetary scientist Patrick Michel on a prize, which mentions the Idefix rover; no new Idefix news), Xinhua and a web search for Tianwen and Zhurong (nothing new; the Tianwen-3 landing site is still expected by year end), the UAE Space Agency (X and a web search; nothing new on Hope), ISRO (nothing on Mars), SpaceX and Elon Musk on X (no Mars or Starship post since 1 November morning), Blue Origin (X and a web search; nothing new), the two GAO protest dockets (both still open, decisions due 21 December 2026 and 1 February 2027), the Planetary Society (an advocacy group; its 30 October newsletter repeats Perseverance's record, nothing new on Mars), SpaceNews (news feed; nothing on Mars), Space.com (only a \"this day in space\" note on the Soviet Mars 1 probe of 1962 and stargazing notes), AMSAT-DL (amateur radio group) and Daniel Estévez's blog (newest post still 26 October, not about Mars). Money and politics: SpacePolicyOnline (Marcia Smith, week-ahead list posted 1 November) says the House and Senate stay in recess until after the 3 November midterm elections and return on 9 November, with all 12 fiscal year 2027 spending bills still unfinished; the stopgap law runs through 11 December. Starship Flight 15 (an Earth-orbit test, not a Mars flight): The Space Road (a news site; updated 1 November) now says an FAA air-traffic planning slide lists the flight no earlier than 12 November, moved from 8 November, citing a 29 October post by the SpaceNotices account on X (which I opened); both call it a planning marker, not a SpaceX date, and no FAA license or SpaceX date is shown [unconfirmed].",
