 dict(when="2026-11-01 02:19", who="Houston Public Media (Michael Adkison), \"They spent more than a year inside a NASA hangar in Houston simulating life on Mars. Now they're finally out\"", kind="news report (Houston public radio and TV)", where="houstonpublicmedia.org (posted 31 Oct, 7:19 p.m. Houston time, 02:19 Israel time 1 Nov)",
  text="The four CHAPEA volunteers came out of NASA's pretend Mars base at Johnson Space Center in Houston on Saturday, 31 October, after 378 days inside. CHAPEA (Crew Health and Performance Exploration Analog) is NASA's yearlong test of how people cope with life on Mars; the base, Mars Dune Alpha, is a 1,700-square-foot 3D-printed habitat. The crew had been inside since 19 October 2025: Ross Elder (commander), Ellen Ellis (medical officer), Matthew Montgomery (science officer) and James Spicer (flight engineer). Johnson's director, Vanessa Wyche, said messages to the crew were delayed 26 to 44 minutes each way, as they would be from Mars. Suzanne Bell, a researcher on the study, said the crew also spent two weeks with no contact at all, to copy the time when the Sun blocks signals between Earth and Mars; the next two weeks are tests and debriefs. Elder called humans on Mars \"not only achievable, but rather inevitable\". NASA plans to fold CHAPEA into a new program, the Moon and Mars Exploration Analog, which could start as soon as August 2027 (Engadget, Mariella Moon, 31 October, says no earlier than August 2027 and that applications are open) [aspirational].",
  evidence="Named reporter; the crew names, 378 days and the ceremony match NASA's own 27 October advisory, and Engadget matches the August 2027 date. NASA had posted no story of its own on the exit when checked", url="https://www.houstonpublicmedia.org/articles/news/nasa/2026/10/31/565803/they-spent-more-than-a-year-inside-a-nasa-hangar-in-houston-simulating-life-on-mars-now-theyre-finally-out/", link_text="open the Houston Public Media story", other="", links=[L("NASA advisory, 27 Oct","https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasa-invites-media-to-crew-completion-of-yearlong-mars-simulation/"),L("Engadget, Mariella Moon, 31 Oct","https://www.engadget.com/2303277/nasa-second-year-long-mars-simulation-mission-ends-today/")]),
 dict(when="2026-10-27", who="Eos (news magazine of the American Geophysical Union), \"Twin Spacecraft Ready to Head for a Martian Escapade\", by Damond Benningfield", kind="news report (science society's magazine)", where="eos.org (first seen in our 1 November check)",
  text="NASA's twin ESCAPADE orbiters, Blue and Gold, have waited near Earth for a year in a looping \"loiter\" orbit and now head for Mars. On 7 November, at its closest pass to Earth, Blue fires its engine for 9.5 minutes and then gets an extra push from Earth's gravity toward Mars; Gold does the same 2 days later. They arrive next September (2027). Robert Lillis of UC Berkeley, the principal investigator (the mission's science lead), says that while waiting they flew about 2 million km down Earth's magnetic tail, farther than any earlier mission. At Mars the pair will study how the solar wind strips away the planet's thin air.",
  evidence="Named reporter quoting the mission's leaders. Berkeley's mission-design page still says only \"November 2026\" and NASA's page \"fall of 2026\", so the 7 and 9 November dates rest on Eos alone [unconfirmed]", url="https://eos.org/articles/twin-spacecraft-ready-to-head-for-a-martian-escapade", link_text="open the Eos story", other="", links=[L("Berkeley ESCAPADE mission design","https://escapade.ssl.berkeley.edu/mission-design/")]),
